The initial implementation of nodejs client based on [Language clients spec](https://docs.google.com/document/d/19zAlnKvMgu075_DDA_k2rY03D_P4wrH6RwBJKJGa8zE/edit#heading) and [Language spec. One pager](https://docs.google.com/document/d/1e0tMosUUFln0o4PzXXwumOa_YFcmR5wDj6ARxPG-tFY/edit).

## Supported features
- inserting data in a row-based form.
  - inserting an array or a stream of JSON objects in `JSONCompactEachRow ` format. We can consider adding support for other formats. Still, IMO we should minimize the number of supported formats in the `insert` operation if we want to convert data into `Native` or `RowBinary` format. We can discuss it later once we have performance testing to compare the throughput of different formats. It didn't tune any streaming settings until we have performance benchmarks to compare different options.
  - inserting a string or a stream of strings is not supported yet. 
- selecting data. see a list of supported formats in https://github.com/ClickHouse/clickhouse-js/pull/1/files#diff-8a7fc60079b51ce7aba6f7e75c059e5b52d7f1eb162c5827dae1ac4d84d2b267
  - as a string
  - as a JSON object
  - a stream of strings or JSON objects.
- sending a command to execute. No response is returned.
- ping a server
- parametrized queries. The query parameters binding is delegated to the ClickHouse.
- instance- and query-level settings
- creates a pool of connections with [Http.Agent](https://nodejs.org/api/http.html#new-agentoptions)
- parsing of ClickHouse errors
- primitive logging will be extended in the future

## High-level overview of the architecture
![2022-07-25_14-41-08](https://user-images.githubusercontent.com/3198181/180779714-386c178c-9973-4c58-948a-79ccafc69d88.png)
`ClickHouse API and Transport` are implemented in a single `ClickHouseClient` class.
`ClickHouseClient` - exposes protocol-agnostic API to communicate to the ClickHouse server. It manages query formatting, data coding/encoding via `DataFormatter` and creating connections via `Connection` factory.

`Cluster manager` and `Connection manager` are not implemented in the MVP phase since we do not support connections to multiple hosts. These components are presented on the diagram for reference. 

Network communication is implemented via the `HttpAdapter` class, which hides an implementation of a specific network protocol. `HttpAdapter` creates a pool of open sockets to a **single host** (uses the nodejs default - `Infinity` number of open sockets). Since the client supports connection to a single host, host health tracking isn't implemented.
`ClickHouseClient` doesn't work with `HttpAdapter` directly. It expects every adapter to implement the `Connection` interface instead.
```typescript
interface Connection {
  ping(): Promise<boolean>;
  close(): Promise<void>;
  select(params: SelectParams): Promise<Stream.Readable>
  command(params: CommandParams): Promise<void>
  insert(params: InsertParams): Promise<void>
}
```

`Data formatter`  restructures language-specific data structures from ClickHouse output / to ClickHouse input formats.

## Client API
The client API surface consists of 4 methods:
### Ping
```typescript
ping: () => Promise<boolean>
```

### Command
```typescript
interface CommandParams {
  query: string;
  clickhouse_settings?: ClickHouseSettings;
  query_params?: Record<string, unknown>;
}
command: (params: CommandParams) => Promise<void>;
```

### Insert
```typescript
interface InsertParams {
  table: string;
  values : ReadonlyArray<any> | Stream.Readable;
  clickhouse_settings?: ClickHouseSettings;
  query_params?: Record<string, unknown>;
}
insert: (params: InsertParams) => Promise<void>;
```

### Select
```typescript
interface SelectParams {
  query: string;
  format?: DataFormat;
  clickhouse_settings?: ClickHouseSettings;
  query_params?: Record<string, unknown>;
}
select: (params: SelectParams) => Promise<Rows>;
```
The result of `select` operation is `Rows` object. I added it to simplify support of different output formats: 
- string
- JSON
- Stream
   - of strings
   - of objects

The interface and functionality of the `Rows` class are discussable and probably don't make much sense for some languages (go, for example).
I'm going to extend it with `summary`, `column names`, `column types`, and `query id` later.
```typescript
interface Rows {
  text: () => Promise<string>;
  json: () => Promise<any>;
  asStream: () => ReadableStream of Row
  
  // not implemented yet
  summary: Summary;
  columnName: string[];
  columnTypes: DataTypes[];
  queryId: string;
}

interface Row {
  text: () => string;
  json: () => any;
}
```

### Demo
see integration tests in `__tests__/integration` folder: https://github.com/ClickHouse/clickhouse-js/tree/fb58b9497f755ab8288aaae93a740995ed503a6f/__tests__/integration

Compression helps reduce the transferred data size at the cost of some CPU overhead. It might be beneficial to enable compression in environments with low network bandwidth.
The client doesn't support extended configuration for the compression settings. The binary switch is sufficient.
Settings:
```typescript
interface Compression {
  // enable compression for the data transferred from ClickHouse to a client. Default value: true.
  read: boolean;
  // enable compression for the data transmitted from a client to the ClickHouse server. Default value: false.
  write: boolean
}
```

We need some performance benchmarks to justify enabling compression by default.

The problem happened due to an unnecessary JSON-streaming parsing in Row. I fixed it by pinning `JSON` format for all JSON-based streaming formats
```
split(function (this: Stream.Transform, row: string) {
  return new Row(row, format)
}),
```

