Addresses [this review comment](https://github.com/ClickHouse/clickhouse-js/pull/911#discussion_r3488458261) on #911.

### Problem

`writeRows` returned a `Writer<readonly T[]>` that ran `writeRow` over the whole array into a single fixed-length `Sink`:

```ts
return (sink, rows) => {
  for (const row of rows) writeRow(sink, row);
};
```

A `Sink` wraps a **fixed-length** buffer. When a row overflows it, `reserve` throws `BufferFull` mid-row and the exception escapes the helper — so the caller can't tell **how many rows actually made it in**, and a result larger than one buffer (or a future unbounded row source) has no way through.

### Fix

`writeRows` now **owns the sink** and returns a **generator** — the encode mirror of how `readRows` catches `NeedMoreData` and commits to the last complete row boundary:

- the caller passes a **`bufferSize`** (not a pre-built sink) and an **`Iterable<T>`** (not a fixed array) — so no commitment to a fixed row count, and a future streaming/infinite source works unchanged;
- catches `BufferFull`, rewinds to the last **complete** row boundary (never a half-written row), `yield`s that buffer, and starts a **fresh `Sink`** for the rows that didn't fit;
- because each flush gets its own buffer, every yielded `Buffer` **stays valid after the generator resumes** — safe to retain or hand to an async sink, no copy needed;
- the trailing partial buffer is yielded too, so the driver loop is a bare `for...of` with nothing to flush afterwards;
- throws if a single row can't fit an empty buffer (it never will) instead of spinning forever.

The driver loop:

```ts
const drive = writeRows(writeRow);
for (const chunk of drive(rows, 64 * 1024)) send(chunk);
```

### Tests

`Rows.write.test.ts` drives the generator, plus cases for the overflow path: byte-identical reassembly across a tiny buffer, yields landing exactly on row boundaries, **a flushed buffer surviving the next iteration** (independent buffers), and the single-row-too-big throw. All 711 package tests pass; typecheck and prettier clean.

