## Description

When the server-side setting [`json_type_escape_dots_in_keys=1`](https://clickhouse.com/docs/sql-reference/data-types/newjson#handling-json-keys-with-dots) is enabled, the server stores JSON keys containing `.` as percent-escaped tokens (`%2E`). The native `clickhouse-client` CLI decodes these escapes on output so the user sees the original key, but `@clickhouse/client` (Node and Web) does not — it returns the keys with the `%2E` sequence intact.

This is the same bug class reported for `clickhouse-connect` in the source issue. `clickhouse-js` is affected because it consumes JSON-family wire formats (`JSONEachRow`, `JSON`, `JSONCompact`, ...) over HTTP and feeds the body straight through `JSON.parse` without post-processing key names. The relevant default lives in [`packages/client-common/src/parse/json_handling.ts`](https://github.com/ClickHouse/clickhouse-js/blob/main/packages/client-common/src/parse/json_handling.ts):

```ts
export const defaultJSONHandling: JSONHandling = {
  parse: JSON.parse,
  stringify: JSON.stringify,
}
```

There is no per-key unescape step anywhere in the result-streaming pipeline, so a column of type `JSON` round-trips as-emitted by the server: dots in the original document come back as `%2E` whenever the server-side setting is on.

## ClickHouse server version

Code analysis only; not verified against a running server. The source bug used ClickHouse 25.9.3.48.

## Reproduction

```ts
import { createClient } from '@clickhouse/client'

const client = createClient({
  clickhouse_settings: {
    // opt-in server-side setting that triggers the bug
    json_type_escape_dots_in_keys: 1,
    // enable JSON type if not already on by default on the target server
    allow_experimental_json_type: 1,
  } as any,
})

await client.command({ query: `DROP TABLE IF EXISTS test_json` })
await client.command({
  query: `CREATE TABLE test_json (data JSON) ENGINE = Memory`,
})

const payload = { 'a.b': 123, c: { 'd.e': 456 } }
await client.insert({
  table: 'test_json',
  values: [{ data: payload }],
  format: 'JSONEachRow',
})

const rs = await client.query({
  query: `SELECT * FROM test_json`,
  format: 'JSONEachRow',
})
const rows = await rs.json<{ data: Record<string, unknown> }>()
console.log(JSON.stringify(rows[0].data))
// Actual:   {"a%2Eb":123,"c":{"d%2Ee":456}}
// Expected: {"a.b":123,"c":{"d.e":456}}
```

The same surface affects `@clickhouse/client-web` because it shares `client-common`'s JSON parsing path.

## Suggested fix

The fidelity gap is in the JSON-column parsing path, not the generic `JSON.parse`. Two possible directions:

1. Recursively walk the value returned for a `JSON`-typed column and apply `decodeURIComponent` (or just `replace(/%2E/g, '.')`) on each key. This matches what the upstream CLI does.
2. Surface a client-side option that mirrors the server setting (e.g., a result-row post-processor for `JSON` columns) so users who deliberately want the escaped form can opt out.

A targeted place to apply the transform would be wherever the result rows are materialized for `JSONEachRow` / `JSON` / `JSONCompact*` formats — after `defaultJSONHandling.parse`, but specifically scoped to `JSON`-typed columns so non-JSON string columns containing literal `%2E` are not silently mutated. Without a column-type-aware step the unescape is unsafe to apply blanket-wide.

