## Description

`parseColumnType` / `parseDateTime64Type` in `packages/client-common/src/parse/column_types.ts` reads the DateTime64 precision by taking **a single character** at a fixed offset and never validates that the parameter token actually ends there. As a result, a malformed type string such as `DateTime64(10)` is silently accepted and reported as precision `1`, instead of raising a `ColumnTypeParseError`.

The same off-by-position logic also corrupts the timezone, because the timezone slice is computed from the same fixed offsets: `DateTime64(12, 'UTC')` yields `timezone: "'UTC"` (with a stray leading quote) and `precision: 1`.

This is the JS/TS counterpart of ClickHouse/clickhouse-rs#514.

Note: ClickHouse itself never emits `DateTime64(10)` in `X-ClickHouse-Format` / RowBinaryWithNamesAndTypes headers, so this is not reachable from a well-behaved server. It matters because `parseColumnType` is **public API** (exported from `@clickhouse/client-common` since #316) and is used by downstream code that may feed it arbitrary type strings; silently returning a *different, valid-looking* type is worse than throwing.

## ClickHouse server version

`26.10.3.7` (the parser bug is purely client-side string parsing; no server interaction is required to reproduce).

## Reproduction

Repo version: `@clickhouse/client@1.25.0` (current `main`).

