## Description

`astToReader` in [`skills/clickhouse-js-node-rowbinary/src/readers/compile.ts:118-123`](https://github.com/ClickHouse/clickhouse-js/blob/main/skills/clickhouse-js-node-rowbinary/src/readers/compile.ts#L118-L123) picks the enum width from the type NAME only:

```ts
case NodeKind.EnumDataType: {
  const map = enumNameMap(node);
  return node.name === "Enum16" ? readEnum16(map) : readEnum8(map);
}
```

The parser keeps a bare `Enum` as `node.name === "Enum"` (`isEnumTypeUpper` in `packages/datatype-parser/src/parser.ts:61` accepts `ENUM`/`ENUM8`/`ENUM16`), so **any** bare `Enum(...)` falls into the `readEnum8` branch, whatever the declared ordinals are.

The server sizes a bare `Enum` from its ordinals:

```sql
SELECT toTypeName(CAST(1000, 'Enum(\'a\' = 1000)'))  -- Enum16('a' = 1000)
SELECT toTypeName(CAST(128,  'Enum(\'a\' = 128)'))   -- Enum16('a' = 128)
SELECT toTypeName(CAST(127,  'Enum(\'a\' = 127)'))   -- Enum8('a' = 127)
```

So for `Enum('a' = 1000)` the server writes 2 bytes and the reader consumes 1. The low byte is resolved against the name map, misses (1000 truncated to `-24`), and the reader returns the stringified int `"-24"`. The leftover high byte then shifts every following column in the row: in a two-column `Enum(...), UInt8` stream the `UInt8` reads `3` instead of `7`. This is a silent wrong-data path, not an error.

Reachable through the public `typeStringToReader(typeStr)` / `astToReader(ast)` API of `@clickhouse/rowbinary` whenever the type string is hand-written rather than taken from a `RowBinaryWithNamesAndTypes` header (the server header always spells out `Enum8`/`Enum16`, so the normal stream path is unaffected). `compile.ts:190-196` already documents hand-written type strings as a supported input for the bare `Enum8`/`Enum16` cases.

The write side is not affected: there is no type-string → writer compiler, callers pick `writeEnum8` / `writeEnum16` themselves.

