### Describe the bug

`@clickhouse/client-web` connects the caller's `abort_signal` to each request with an assignment:

```ts
// packages/client-web/src/connection/web_connection.ts:244-249 (main 883042a)
if (params?.abort_signal !== undefined) {
  params.abort_signal.onabort = () => {
    isAborted = true;
    abortController.abort();
  };
}
```

An `AbortSignal` has only one `onabort` handler. This causes two problems:

1. When one signal is given to two or more requests that run at the same time, `abort()` cancels only the request that started last. The other requests continue and resolve normally.
2. The client replaces an `onabort` handler that the caller set on the signal. The caller's handler does not run.

This applies to `query`, `command`, `exec` and `insert`, because all of them send their requests through `WebConnection.request()`. The Node.js client (`@clickhouse/client`) uses `addEventListener("abort", …)` (`packages/client-node/src/connection/node_base_connection.ts:508`) and cancels all the requests.

### Steps to reproduce

1. Make one `AbortController`.
2. Start two concurrent requests with `abort_signal: controller.signal` (`SELECT sleep(2)`).
3. Call `controller.abort()` after 300 ms.
4. Request A resolves after ~2 s. Only request B rejects.

### Expected behaviour

`controller.abort()` cancels every in-progress request that uses this signal, and a handler that the caller set on `signal.onabort` still runs. This is the `AbortSignal` contract (a signal can have many listeners; `onabort` belongs to the code that owns the signal), the documented meaning of `abort_signal` ("AbortSignal instance to cancel a request in progress", `packages/client-common/src/client.ts:55`), and the behavior of the Node.js client.

### Code example

<details><summary>Reproduction program (JavaScript, 28 lines)</summary>

```js
import { createClient, ClickHouseLogLevel } from "@clickhouse/client-web";

const client = createClient({
  url: process.env.CLICKHOUSE_URL ?? "http://localhost:8123",
  log: { level: ClickHouseLogLevel.OFF },
});

const controller = new AbortController();
const t0 = Date.now();
const run = (name) =>
  client
    .command({ query: "SELECT sleep(2)", abort_signal: controller.signal })
    .then(() => `${name}: resolved after ${Date.now() - t0} ms`)
    .catch((e) => `${name}: rejected after ${Date.now() - t0} ms: ${e.message}`);

const p = [run("A"), run("B")];
setTimeout(() => controller.abort(), 300);
for (const line of await Promise.all(p)) console.log(line);

// The caller's own onabort handler is replaced:
const c2 = new AbortController();
let callerHandlerRan = false;
c2.signal.onabort = () => { callerHandlerRan = true; };
const req = client.command({ query: "SELECT sleep(1)", abort_signal: c2.signal }).catch((e) => e.message);
setTimeout(() => c2.abort(), 200);
console.log(await req, "| caller onabort ran:", callerHandlerRan);

await client.close();
```

</details>

