## Description

ClickHouse accepts `Enum` elements written without a number and assigns the numbers itself (`Enum8('a', 'b')` is stored as `Enum8('a' = 1, 'b' = 2)`; see [Enum docs](https://clickhouse.com/docs/sql-reference/data-types/enum)). Two places in this repo assume every element carries an explicit `= <number>`:

**1. `parseColumnType` (public API, `@clickhouse/client` / `@clickhouse/client-web` / `@clickhouse/client-common`) throws.**

`packages/client-common/src/parse/column_types.ts` — `parseEnumType` walks the body expecting `'name' = N` for each element (`i += 4; // skip ' = ' and the first digit`, lines 417 and 430), then `pushEnumIndex` runs `parseInt` on whatever follows. With no `= N`, the slice is not a number and it throws `ColumnTypeParseError: Expected Enum index to be a valid number`. Every auto-assigned form fails, including nested ones.

**2. The RowBinary codec (`@clickhouse/rowbinary`, `skills/clickhouse-js-node-rowbinary`) silently decodes the wrong value.**

`packages/datatype-parser`'s `parseEnumValues` is explicit-only (`parser.ts:286-288`: "Returns false (caller restores) for auto-assigned or otherwise non-trivial enums"), so `Enum8('a', 'b')` comes back as a generic `DataType` node named `Enum8` rather than an `EnumDataType`. `astToReader` then hits `case \"Enum8\": return readEnum8(new Map())` (`src/readers/compile.ts:193-196`) and the empty map makes the reader return the stringified underlying int. A hand-written type string for a headerless `RowBinary` stream decodes `'b'` as `\"2\"` — no error, just wrong data.

This is only reachable from type strings the application writes by hand (`parseColumnType(...)`, a hand-built reader for `RowBinary` without a header). Types that come from the server (`RowBinaryWithNamesAndTypes` / `Native` headers, `DESCRIBE`) are always normalized with explicit numbers, so the common paths are unaffected.

## ClickHouse server version

26.10.3.7 (`SELECT toTypeName(defaultValueOfTypeName('Enum8(''a'', ''b'')'))` -> `Enum8('a' = 1, 'b' = 2)`).

