## Suggested fix

Assign the implicit numbers the way the server does (`autoAssignNumberForEnum` in `src/DataTypes/DataTypeEnum.cpp`): an element without a number at position `i` gets `base + i`, where `base` is 1, or the first element's number if the first element has one. The server accepts a mixed list only when all elements after the first have a number, or none of them do — other mixed lists (`Enum8('a' = 5, 'b', 'c' = 10, 'd')`) and unquoted names stay errors.

- `packages/client-common/src/parse/column_types.ts:388-486` (`parseEnumType`): after reading a closing quote, make the ` = <int>` part optional instead of unconditionally skipping 4 chars, and fill in the missing numbers before building `values`.
- `packages/datatype-parser/src/parser.ts:286-330` (`parseEnumValues`): accept a value-less element and assign its number, so `Enum8('a', 'b')` produces an `EnumDataType` node and `skills/clickhouse-js-node-rowbinary/src/readers/compile.ts:118-122` gets a populated name map. That also removes the silent `readEnum8(new Map())` fallback for this case.

Note `Enum8('a' = -3, 'b', 'c')` is valid on the server (`-3, -2, -1`), but `parseEnumType`'s `pushEnumIndex` also rejects any negative index (`index < 0`, line 462) — worth fixing in the same pass.

Related: #1130 (width of a bare `Enum(...)`) — if the width is derived from the element numbers, the implicit numbers have to be assigned first: `Enum('a' = 127, 'b')` is `Enum16` on the server.

