## Description

`unescapeEnumName` in `packages/client-common/src/parse/column_types.ts` (used by the exported `parseColumnType` / `parseEnumType`) only decodes `\n`, `\t`, `\r`, `\\` and `\'`. Every other escape falls into the `default` branch, which drops the backslash and keeps the following character verbatim.

The server, however, also writes `\0` (NUL), `\b` (backspace) and `\f` (form feed) when it renders an `Enum8`/`Enum16` type name (`writeAnyEscapedString`). For those three, the current decoder returns the literal ASCII characters `0`, `b`, `f` instead of the control characters — silently, with no error. So the label the client reports is not the label the server stores, and a lookup/insert with the real label would not match.

The `\0`-followed-by-a-digit case is the same one reported for the .NET client: the server writes the label `a` + NUL + `12b` as `Enum8('a\012b' = 1)`, which decodes here to `a012b`.

Scope: this only affects the **deprecated** `parseColumnType` export (`@clickhouse/client`, `@clickhouse/client-web`, `@clickhouse/client-common`). The current `@clickhouse/datatype-parser` (`parseDataType`, used by the RowBinary reader) decodes all of these correctly — I verified it side by side in the same run, and it matches the server for every case below. Filing this because `parseColumnType` is still exported public API.

## ClickHouse server version

26.10.3.7 (verified against a running server, type names fetched with `FORMAT TabSeparatedRaw` so the TSV layer does not re-escape them).

