### Describe the bug

A query can fail after ClickHouse (25.11+) has sent `200 OK` and part of the body. The server then writes the in-band exception block and closes the connection without the last HTTP chunk. `ResultSet.stream()` of `@clickhouse/client-web` raises the `ClickHouseError` only if the reader has consumed the body up to the exception block when the connection closes.

In Chromium, a reader that is slower than the network gets `TypeError: network error` instead. A short `await` in the read loop (5 ms every 10 batches) is sufficient. Firefox raises the `ClickHouseError`. The 1.13.0 entry in `packages/client-web/CHANGELOG.md:504` says that mid-stream exceptions "are now handled correctly". In Chromium, this is true only for a reader that keeps up.

`ResultSet.stream()`, code example below (error at `number = 1000000`, more than 100 MB of output before it), every run:

| Read loop | Chromium 156 | Firefox 157 |
|---|---|---|
| no delay | `ClickHouseError` 4/5, `TypeError` 1/5 | `ClickHouseError` 5/5 |
| `await sleep(5)` every 10 batches (two series) | `TypeError` 8/10, `ClickHouseError` 2/10 | `ClickHouseError` 10/10 |
| `await sleep(200)` after the first batch only | `ClickHouseError` 5/5 | `ClickHouseError` 5/5 |

The raw `response.body` (from `client.exec()`, no client parsing) shows that the browser itself drops the bytes. Same query, `FORMAT JSONEachRow`, error at `number = 300000` (about 18 MB):

| Read loop | Chromium 156: exception block in the bytes read | Firefox 157 |
|---|---|---|
| no delay | 3/3 | 3/3 |
| `await sleep(5)` every 10 reads | 1/3 (the other runs read 16.7 MB and 17.3 MB, then `TypeError`) | 2/2 |
| `await sleep(2000)` after the first read | 0/5 (only 64-256 KB read, then `TypeError`) | 6/6 |

All raw reads end with a `TypeError`, because the chunked framing is cut. The only difference is whether the exception block arrives before the error.

### Steps to reproduce

1. Put the code example in `packages/client-web/__tests__/integration/midstream_chromium.test.ts` (a local ClickHouse 25.11+ on `127.0.0.1:8123`).
2. Run `cd packages/client-web && TEST_MODE=integration BROWSER=chromium npx vitest run midstream_chromium`.
3. Most runs end with `TypeError: network error`. With `BROWSER=firefox`, every run ends with `ClickHouseError` code `395`.

### Expected behaviour

The `ClickHouseError` (code `395`, message `boom`) that Firefox and a fast Chromium reader get. The server sends the complete exception block: Firefox reads it from the same responses, and `curl` gets it too (`curl: (18) transfer closed with outstanding read data remaining`, body ends with `\r\n__exception__\r\n<tag>\r\nCode: 395. DB::Exception: boom ...`).

