<sub>Found by automated analysis (Polyglot AI) while working on the triage of #976. Verified against a live server.</sub>

## Summary

With Node.js 24 and 26, an insert from an object-mode stream (`JSONEachRow` and the other JSON formats) with non-ASCII text can stop after about 64 KiB of the request body. The client stops sending, and the insert fails with a timeout. Node.js 22 is not affected, and ASCII text is not affected.

Found by the new integration test "inserts rows with multi-byte characters" in #1143. On ClickHouse Cloud, it failed 3 of 3 times with Node.js 24.22.2 and 26.13.1, and the server reported:

```text
Timeout exceeded while reading from socket (peer: ..., local: ...:8124, 30000 ms)
```

It passed on Cloud with Node.js 22.23.4. In CI with a local ClickHouse, it passed on all versions, because the stall depends on the timing of the writes.

## Affected versions

| Node.js | Result |
|---|---|
| 22.23.4 | not affected |
| 24.22.2, 24.22.3 | stalls |
| 26.13.1 | stalls |

`@clickhouse/client` 1.25.0 and `main`. Plain HTTP and HTTPS are both affected.

## Cause

The client encodes each row to a JSON string (`mapStream` in `packages/client-node/src/utils/stream.ts`) and pipes the strings into `http.ClientRequest`. Since Node.js 24, `ClientRequest` collects the writes of one tick into one chunk (nodejs/node#64987):

- `write()` returns `false` when the collected data reaches 64 KiB. It counts strings in **bytes** (`Buffer.byteLength`).
- The request emits `'drain'` when its socket emits `'drain'`. The socket counts strings in **characters**.

A non-ASCII string has more bytes than characters. So the request asks the writer to wait, but the socket is never full and never emits `'drain'`, and the pipe waits for ever. At the stall, the request reports `writableNeedDrain: true` and `writableLength: 0`, and the socket is idle.

## Reproduction without the client

```js
import http from "node:http";
import Stream from "node:stream";

const server = http.createServer((req, res) => {
  req.resume();
  req.on("end", () => res.end("ok"));
});
await new Promise((r) => server.listen(0, "127.0.0.1", r));

const req = http.request({
  host: "127.0.0.1",
  port: server.address().port,
  method: "POST",
});
req.on("response", () => {
  console.log(process.version, "complete");
  process.exit(0);
});
// Use `name-${i}` (ASCII), or Buffer.from(...), and it completes.
const rows = Array.from({ length: 50_000 }, (_, i) => `{"name":"ééééé-${i}"}\n`);
Stream.pipeline(Stream.Readable.from(rows), req, () => {});

setTimeout(() => {
  console.log(process.version, "STALL", {
    reqNeedDrain: req.writableNeedDrain,
    reqLength: req.writableLength,
    socketNeedDrain: req.socket.writableNeedDrain,
    bytesWritten: req.socket.bytesWritten,
  });
  process.exit(1);
}, 3000);
```

Output:

```text
v22.23.4 complete
v24.22.3 STALL { reqNeedDrain: true, reqLength: 0, socketNeedDrain: false, bytesWritten: 65547 }
v26.13.1 STALL { reqNeedDrain: true, reqLength: 0, socketNeedDrain: false, bytesWritten: 65547 }
```

## Fix

The client writes the encoded rows as Buffers, so the request and the socket count the same bytes. See the PR that references this issue.

A raw-format stream (CSV, TSV) that sends strings, for example after `setEncoding()`, can probably hit the same stall. Most raw streams send Buffers. This case is not tested.

## Describe the bug

`ClickHouseError.type` is often wrong, and `ClickHouseError.message` is cut short. `parseError` takes the first `(UPPERCASE)` group in the server message as the type. Many server messages have such a group before the real type:

- a type name, such as `Nullable(JSON)`;
- a token of a syntax error, such as `failed at position 38 (TABLE)`;
- `(NULL)`.

For a wrapped error, such as `CANNOT_EXTRACT_TABLE_STRUCTURE` around `INCORRECT_DATA`, `code` is the outer code but `type` is the inner type.

## Steps to reproduce

```ts
import { createClient } from "@clickhouse/client";
const client = createClient();

try {
  await client.query({ query: "SELECT tupleElement(1, 'a')" });
} catch (e) {
  console.log(e.code, e.type, e.message);
  // 43 JSON First argument for function tupleElement must be Tuple, Nullable(Tuple), QBit, JSON, Nullable
}

try {
  await client.command({ query: "ALTER TABLE x MOVE PART '0_1_1_0' TO TABLE y" });
} catch (e) {
  console.log(e.code, e.type); // 62 TABLE
}

try {
  await client.query({ query: `DESCRIBE TABLE format(JSONEachRow, '{"x":1,"x":2}')` });
} catch (e) {
  console.log(e.code, e.type); // 636 INCORRECT_DATA
}
```

## Expected behaviour

`type` is the name of the error code: `ILLEGAL_TYPE_OF_ARGUMENT`, `SYNTAX_ERROR` and `CANNOT_EXTRACT_TABLE_STRUCTURE`. `message` is the full message.

## Notes

- The server writes the real type at the end of the exception: before ` (version ...)`, or at the end of the text for a request with a `session_id`, and before `, Stack trace (` with the `stacktrace` setting.
- #819 (cluster-mode errors, #39) made the message match lazy, so the first group became the type.
- The upstream SQL test harness (#1169) lists 101 tests that fail for this reason (group `error-type-from-message`).
- Affects `@clickhouse/client` and `@clickhouse/client-web`. Seen with ClickHouse 26.10.4.207.

### Description

`formatQueryParams` (`packages/client-common/src/data_formatter/format_query_params.ts`) escapes **every** string value in `query_params` the same way, regardless of the type declared in the placeholder: `'` becomes `\'`, `\` becomes `\\`, and tab / newline / carriage return become their two-character `\t`, `\n`, `\r` escapes.

That is correct for `{x:String}` — the server unescapes the value. For `{x:Identifier}` the server takes the substituted text **as-is**, so the escape backslashes become part of the identifier name. The query succeeds with no error; it just names a different identifier.

The client has no way to know the declared type at format time: `query_params` is a plain `Record<string, unknown>` and the type only appears inside the query text (`{x:Identifier}`), so the escaping is applied unconditionally in `formatQueryParamsInternal`.

Practical impact: an alias comes back with extra backslashes, and reads of JSON subcolumns / dynamic column names (`attributes.{key:Identifier}`) silently return `NULL`, because the key with the added backslash does not exist. Plain names are unaffected, which makes this easy to miss.

Note: unlike clickhouse-connect, a backtick in the name is *not* escaped here, so `` a`b `` round-trips fine. The affected characters are `'`, backslash, tab, newline and carriage return.

Relayed from https://github.com/ClickHouse/clickhouse-connect/issues/1243 (same root cause, different client).

### ClickHouse server version

`26.10.4.207` (local HTTP, anonymous). Verified with a real query, see below.

### Reproduction

Dropped into `packages/client-common/__tests__/integration/` and run with `npm run test:node:integration -- --run <file>`:

```ts
import { describe, it, expect, beforeAll, afterAll } from "vitest";
import { type ClickHouseClient } from "@clickhouse/client-common";
import { createTestClient } from "../utils";

describe("Identifier query params", () => {
  let client: ClickHouseClient;
  beforeAll(() => {
    client = createTestClient();
  });
  afterAll(async () => {
    await client.close();
  });

  it("uses the identifier name verbatim", async () => {
    for (const name of ["plain", "a`b", "e\\f", "it's", "x\ty"]) {
      const rs = await client.query({
        query: "SELECT 1 AS {name:Identifier}",
        format: "JSON",
        query_params: { name },
      });
      const json = (await rs.json()) as { meta: { name: string }[] };
      expect(json.meta[0].name).toBe(name); // alias must equal the bound name
    }
  });
});
```

Actual (printing instead of asserting, so every case is visible — all strings shown JSON-escaped, i.e. a single backslash prints as two):

```text
input="plain"  alias="plain"    match=true
input="a`b"    alias="a`b"      match=true
input="e\\f"   alias="e\\\\f"   match=false   <- one backslash became two
input="it's"   alias="it\\'s"   match=false   <- backslash inserted before the quote
input="x\ty"   alias="x\\ty"    match=false   <- the tab became the two chars backslash + t
```

Expected: `match=true` for all five inputs.

The same mechanism breaks subcolumn reads:

```ts
await client.command({
  query: "CREATE TABLE repro (attributes JSON) ENGINE = MergeTree ORDER BY tuple()",
});
await client.insert({
  table: "repro",
  values: [{ attributes: { plain: 1, "it's": 4 } }],
  format: "JSONEachRow",
});
// returns null instead of 4, because the server looks up a key with an extra backslash
await client.query({
  query: "SELECT attributes.{name:Identifier} FROM repro",
  query_params: { name: "it's" },
  format: "JSON",
});
```

### Suggested fix

The escaping lives in the `typeof value === "string"` branch of `formatQueryParamsInternal` in `packages/client-common/src/data_formatter/format_query_params.ts` (the `switch (value.charCodeAt(i))` block). Values bound to an `Identifier` placeholder should be sent through unchanged.

Unlike clickhouse-connect — whose `bind_query` already knows each placeholder's declared type — this client discards that information, so a fix likely needs the query text at format time: scan the query for `{<name>:Identifier}` (ClickHouse itself allows whitespace around the name and type) and skip escaping for those keys. The call site that would need the query passed down is `transformUrl` / `serializeQueryParamsForUrl` in `packages/client-common/src/utils/url.ts` (around the line that pushes the `param_<key>` entry), plus the equivalent multipart path in `packages/client-common/src/utils/multipart.ts`.

I have not attempted the fix.
